<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a Right Triangle Using Inradius and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>A right triangle has an inradius of <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and a semiperimeter of <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]].')} /> Apply <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The triangle encloses 24 square units of flat area. This is the complete method the calculator automates: two measurements, one multiplication, one answer.</p>
  <p>The inradius is the radius of the circle that fits snugly inside the triangle, touching all three sides. The semiperimeter is half the distance around the triangle. Together they are enough to determine the area because the circle's radius acts as the height of three smaller triangles whose bases are the triangle's sides. This identity holds for every non-degenerate triangle, not only right triangles; what is special about right triangles is the fast way the inradius itself can be computed from the three sides. It is most useful when the incircle has been measured directly, or when the inradius and semiperimeter are already known from earlier work.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the incircle and inradius -->
<section id="sec-incircle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is an Incircle in Geometry?</h2>
  <p>The notation throughout is: <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> are the vertices and angles with <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} />; <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the two legs; <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse opposite <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />; <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the inradius; <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> is the incenter; <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is the semiperimeter; <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> is the perimeter; and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area, used everywhere so it is never confused with angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Definition of an Incircle</h3>
  <p>An incircle is the largest circle that fits inside a triangle while touching all three of its sides. It lies entirely inside because each side of the triangle is a tangent line to the circle: the circle reaches the side at exactly one point and stays on the inside of it. A circle that merely lies near a side does not count; the incircle must be tangent to each side, touching it at a single point and then turning back into the interior. Every non-degenerate triangle has exactly one such circle, and it has a unique center, because the three lines are not parallel and their geometry pins the circle down completely.</p>

  <h3>What Is an Inradius?</h3>
  <p>The inradius is the distance from the incenter to any side of the triangle. If <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> is the incenter and <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} /> is the point where the incircle touches a side, then <span set:html={rich('[[r=ID]].')} /> The circle's radius is the shortest distance from its center to the tangent side, and that shortest distance is the same for all three sides, which is why the circle is tangent to all three rather than merely near them.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Inradius Perpendicular to Each Side?</h3>
  <p>A basic property of circles says that at every point where a line touches a circle, the radius drawn to that point meets the line at a right angle. So if <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} /> is the touchpoint on side <span set:html={rich('[[AB]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[ID\\perp AB]].')} /> The same holds for the other two sides. This perpendicularity is not decorative: it is what lets each radius act as the height of a smaller triangle when the area formula is derived, since a triangle's height must be perpendicular to its base.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Incenter</h3>
  <p>The incenter <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> is the point where the triangle's three internal angle bisectors meet. Each angle bisector is the set of points equally distant from the two sides forming that angle, so a point sitting on all three bisectors is equally distant from all three sides, and it must lie inside the triangle. That common distance is exactly the inradius, and the circle centered at <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> with radius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> touches each side once: the incircle.</p>

  <h3>Is the Inradius the Same as the Circumradius?</h3>
  <p>No. The inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the radius of the circle inside the triangle, while the circumradius <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> is the radius of the circle that passes through all three vertices, the circumcircle. For a right triangle the circumcircle's center is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{R=\\frac{c}{2}}]]]')} />. The two radii describe completely different circles: one is inscribed in the triangle, the other is drawn around it. Mixing them up is one of the most common errors in this topic, so keep them separate: <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> for inside, <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> for around.</p>

