</section>

<!-- Section 3: Understanding the semiperimeter -->
<section id="sec-semiperimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Semiperimeter of a Triangle?</h2>

  <h3>Definition of Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>The semiperimeter is half the total perimeter of a triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. It is a length, like any side, and it earns its place in the area formula because it combines all three sides into one number.</p>

  <h3>Difference Between Perimeter and Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>The perimeter is the full distance around the triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />, while the semiperimeter is half of that, <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{P}{2}]].')} /> For a 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5=12]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12}{2}=6]].')} /> The semiperimeter is not the length of the boundary; it is half of it, and feeding the full perimeter into <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> would double the true area.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Semiperimeter Used in the Area Formula?</h3>
  <p>Connecting the incenter to the three vertices divides the triangle into three smaller triangles, each with perpendicular height <span set:html={rich('[[r]].')} /> Their bases are <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, so their combined area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12r(a+b+c)]].')} /> Since <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />, the half-perimeter is already built into <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]].')} /> The next section derives this fully.</p>

  <h3>Can the Semiperimeter Be Smaller Than One Side?</h3>
  <p>No. The triangle inequality says that for any triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}>c]].')} /> The same argument applies to the other two sides: <span set:html={rich('[[s>a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s>b]].')} /> The semiperimeter is always larger than each individual side. This is a quick sanity check on inputs: if a claimed semiperimeter is smaller than any side, the numbers cannot describe a real triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Deriving the main area formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Area of a Triangle Equal Inradius Times Semiperimeter?</h2>
  <p>Start from the standard triangle area rule: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12\\times\\text{base}\\times\\text{height}]].')} /> Drawing segments from the incenter <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> to the three vertices divides the original triangle into three smaller triangles. Each smaller triangle has a base equal to one side of the original triangle and a perpendicular height equal to the inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, because the radius meets each side at a right angle. This works for every non-degenerate triangle; for this calculator the triangle is right-angled, but the area identity itself is fully general.</p>

  <h3>Area of the First Smaller Triangle</h3>
  <p>The smaller triangle standing on side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> has base <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_1=\\frac{ar}{2}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Area of the Second Smaller Triangle</h3>
  <p>The smaller triangle standing on side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> has base <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_2=\\frac{br}{2}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Area of the Third Smaller Triangle</h3>
  <p>The smaller triangle standing on side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> has base <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_3=\\frac{cr}{2}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Add the Three Areas</h3>
  <p>The three smaller triangles tile the original without overlap, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=K_1+K_2+K_3]].')} /> Substituting: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ar}{2}+\\frac{br}{2}+\\frac{cr}{2}]].')} /> Factor out <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{r}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{r(a+b+c)}{2}]].')} /> Recall <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />, therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=r\\times s}]]]')} />. Every transition is justified: the tiling is exact, the height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is common, and the semiperimeter absorbs the division by two.</p>

  <h3>Why Does This Formula Not Need Another Division by Two?</h3>
  <p>Because the one-half is already folded into the semiperimeter. The three halves combine into <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{r(a+b+c)}{2}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is exactly half the perimeter, so dividing by two a second time would quarter the correct expression. Calculate <span set:html={rich('[[r\\times s]]')} /> and stop there; a further halving is a mistake, not a correction.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('incircle-three-triangles.svg')} alt="Incircle dividing triangle ABC into three smaller triangles with height r and bases a, b, c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Three smaller triangles with common height r and bases a, b, c: their areas ar/2 + br/2 + cr/2 add to rs.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: The right-triangle inradius shortcut -->
<section id="sec-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Inradius Using the Three Side Lengths</h2>
  <p>When the three sides are known, the inradius of a right triangle follows a shortcut that avoids drawing the incircle at all: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}}]]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the perpendicular legs and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. This is one of the handiest facts in right-triangle geometry.</p>

  <h3>Deriving the Right Triangle Inradius Formula</h3>
  <p>The derivation uses the equal tangent-segment theorem: from a common external point, the two tangent segments drawn to a circle have equal lengths. Let the tangent lengths from the three vertices be <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> from vertex <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} /> from vertex <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[z]]')} /> from vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]].')} /> At the right angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />, the incircle's two tangents run along the perpendicular legs and each has length <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, forming a small square with the two radii, so <span set:html={rich('[[z=r]].')} /> The sides are then <span set:html={rich('[[a=y+r]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=x+r]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=x+y]].')} /> Subtract: <span set:html={rich('[[a+b-c=(y+r)+(x+r)-(x+y)]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a+b-c=2r]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}}]]]')} />. The square at the right angle is why this shortcut belongs to right triangles alone.</p>

  <h3>Example Using a 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{3+4-5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=1}]]]')} />. The incircle's radius is exactly 1, no construction needed.</p>

  <h3>Example Using a 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{5+12-13}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=2}]]]')} />. Again, the inradius drops out of the side lengths directly.</p>

  <h3>Finding the Semiperimeter at the Same Time</h3>
  <p>With the same three sides, compute <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> alongside <span set:html={rich('[[r]].')} /> For the 5-12-13 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{5+12+13}{2}=15]]')} />, and then <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs=2\\times15=30]].')} /> One pass over the three sides supplies both calculator inputs and the area itself.</p>

  <h3>When Should You Use This Shortcut?</h3>
  <p>Use it whenever the three sides are known but the incircle has not been measured or drawn: textbook exercises, construction dimensions, and survey data usually give side lengths, not an inradius. The shortcut converts them to <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> in one line. When the inradius is the measured quantity instead, enter it directly and skip the conversion.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-tangent-segments.svg')} alt="3-4-5 triangle showing equal tangent segments and the inradius shortcut r = (a+b-c)/2 = 1" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Tangent segments on a 3-4-5 triangle: equal lengths from each vertex give r = (3 + 4 - 5)/2 = 1.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area From Inradius and Semiperimeter Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator needs exactly two inputs. The walkthrough below follows the on-screen order, from known measurements to a verified result.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the inradius r.</strong> The radius of the inscribed circle, the perpendicular distance from the incenter to any side.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the semiperimeter s.</strong> Half the total perimeter of the triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>If the three sides are known, calculate r using <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]].')} /></strong> Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculate s using <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]].')} /></strong></li>
    <li><strong>Enter the inradius in the first field.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Enter the semiperimeter in the second field.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible length units.</strong> Both measurements must use the same unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that both values are positive.</strong> Zero or negative lengths describe no triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Review the result.</strong> The area appears in the square units matching the inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that the answer is expressed in square units.</strong> Metres give square metres; centimetres give square centimetres.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the mathematical solution.</strong> It shows the substitution and the arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset when beginning a new calculation.</strong> It clears both fields so the next calculation starts clean.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator does not ask for the three side lengths: the inradius and the semiperimeter already contain everything the formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> needs.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: Four foundational worked examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Area Calculation Using Inradius and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Four worked examples build the method from the familiar upward. Each one computes the semiperimeter and the inradius from the three sides, applies <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> with every substitution shown, and verifies with the legs formula.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> Step 1: semiperimeter, <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6]].')} /> Step 2: inradius, <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{3+4-5}{2}=1]].')} /> Step 3: apply the formula, <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Example 2: 6-8-10 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{6+8+10}{2}=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{6+8-10}{2}=2]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}=24]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Example 3: 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
