<!-- Section 9: Relationship between inradius, semiperimeter, and the three sides -->
<section id="sec-relationship" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Are Inradius and Semiperimeter Related to the Three Sides?</h2>
  <p>In a right triangle the inradius and semiperimeter are not independent of the sides; they are built from them. Starting from <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />, add and subtract the two expressions. Adding gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s+r=a+b}]]]')} />, and subtracting gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s-r=c}]]]')} />. These two identities are specific to right triangles under the given notation, and they turn the two calculator inputs into a surprising amount of information about the triangle itself.</p>

  <h3>Recovering the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>From <span set:html={rich('[[s-r=c]]')} />, the hypotenuse can be recovered when <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> are known. For <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[c=12-2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=10}]]]')} />. Two inputs thus reveal the longest side without any further measurement.</p>

  <h3>Recovering the Sum of the Two Legs</h3>
  <p>From <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=s+r]]')} />, the legs' sum follows directly. For <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=14]].')} /> The sum alone cannot identify each leg individually; the individual lengths need one more piece of information.</p>

  <h3>Recovering the Product of the Two Legs</h3>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} />, it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{ab=2rs}]]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[ab=48]].')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=14]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[ab=48]]')} />, the two legs are 6 and 8: <span set:html={rich('[[6+8=14]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[6\\times8=48]].')} /> The two legs can be interchanged without changing the triangle's area.</p>

  <h3>Recovering Both Leg Lengths</h3>
  <p>The sum and product make <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> the two roots of <span set:html={rich('[[t^2-(s+r)t+2rs=0]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a,b=\\frac{(s+r)\\pm\\sqrt{(s+r)^2-8rs}}{2}}]]]')} />. The two signs correspond to the two possible leg assignments. With <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a,b=\\frac{14\\pm\\sqrt{196-192}}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a,b=\\frac{14\\pm2}{2}]]')} />, legs 6 and 8. This is an advanced mathematical deduction, not an additional calculator feature.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Input validation and geometric consistency -->
<section id="sec-valid" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Inradius and Semiperimeter Form a Valid Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The calculator multiplies two inputs, but not every positive pair of lengths describes a right triangle. The checks below separate arithmetic from geometry.</p>

  <h3>Basic Input Conditions</h3>
  <p>The inputs must satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[r>0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s>0]].')} /> For a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[s>r]]')} /> is also required, because <span set:html={rich('[[c=s-r]]')} /> and the hypotenuse must be positive. These three conditions are necessary but not sufficient.</p>

  <h3>An Advanced Right-Triangle Consistency Check</h3>
  <p>From <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=s+r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[ab=2rs]]')} />, the two legs are real exactly when the discriminant <span set:html={rich('[[\\Delta=(s+r)^2-8rs]]')} /> is non-negative. Simplified: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\Delta=s^2-6rs+r^2}]]]')} />. For real leg lengths, <span set:html={rich('[[\\Delta\\geq0]].')} /> Since <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> must exceed <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, the equivalent right-triangle condition is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\frac{s}{r}\\geq3+2\\sqrt2}]]]')} />, or <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\frac{r}{s}\\leq3-2\\sqrt2}]]]')} />. Equality occurs for an isosceles right triangle, the tightest possible fit. This is an advanced mathematical compatibility test, clearly separated from the basic checks above.</p>

  <h3>Valid Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />, the ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{s}{r}=6]].')} /> Since <span set:html={rich('[[6>3+2\\sqrt2]]')} /> (about 5.83), the values can describe a valid right triangle, and the corresponding sides are 6, 8, and 10.</p>

  <h3>Incompatible Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=10]]')} />, the ratio is <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{s}{r}=5]]')} />, below the required threshold of about 5.83. This pair cannot describe a right triangle, even though multiplying the inputs produces a numerical result. Calculating <span set:html={rich('[[rs]]')} /> and confirming the measurements describe a right triangle are two different tasks. This guide does not claim the calculator performs this check automatically.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Comparing with Heron's formula -->
<section id="sec-heron" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Does the Inradius Formula Compare With Heron's Formula?</h2>
  <p>Heron's formula computes the area from all three sides: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}}]]]')} />. It uses the three side lengths and the semiperimeter, it works for any non-degenerate triangle, and it does not need the inradius as a separate input. Compare it with <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=rs}]]]')} />. The two are close relatives: the semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> appears in both, and proving they agree is one of the cleanest ways to see that <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> is correct.</p>

  <h3>When Is the Inradius Formula Easier?</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> is especially convenient when the inradius and semiperimeter are already known: a single multiplication, no square roots, no factoring of side differences. When an incircle has been measured, or when earlier steps already produced <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, this is the shortest path to the area.</p>

  <h3>When Is Heron's Formula More Useful?</h3>
  <p>Heron's formula is the better choice when the three side lengths are known but the inradius has not been calculated. It reaches the area directly from <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> without the detour through <span set:html={rich('[[r]].')} /> Neither formula is universally more accurate; they are exact on consistent inputs, and they disagree only when the inputs are inconsistent.</p>

  <h3>Verify Both Formulas Using a 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]].')} /> Using the inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6=6]].')} /> Using Heron's formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6(6-3)(6-4)(6-5)}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6\\times3\\times2\\times1}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6}]]]')} />. The results agree because both formulas measure the same region; they are two expressions of one area, and agreement between them is a strong sign the arithmetic is right.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Consistency with the legs formula -->
<section id="sec-legs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Inradius Formula Produce the Same Area as the Legs Formula?</h2>
  <p>Two formulas claim the same area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]].')} /> Writing <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> in terms of the sides gives <span set:html={rich('[[rs=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}\\times\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[rs=\\frac{(a+b-c)(a+b+c)}{4}]].')} /> The numerator is a difference of squares: <span set:html={rich('[[rs=\\frac{(a+b)^2-c^2}{4}]].')} /> Apply the Pythagorean theorem <span set:html={rich('[[c^2=a^2+b^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[rs=\\frac{a^2+2ab+b^2-a^2-b^2}{4}]]')} />, therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{rs=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />. The two formulas are identical on every right triangle; they cannot disagree on consistent inputs.</p>
  <p>Check with a 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[rs=6]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{ab}{2}=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]].')} /> With a 6-8-10 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[rs=24]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{ab}{2}=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}=24]].')} /> The familiar legs formula and the inradius formula are the same quantity wearing different clothes.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Area From Inradius and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Most wrong answers come from mislabelled inputs rather than bad arithmetic. The fifteen mistakes below cover nearly every one.</p>

  <h3>1. Confusing the Inradius With the Circumradius</h3>
  <p>The inradius is the circle inside; the circumradius is the circle around. In a 6-8-10 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> but <span set:html={rich('[[R=\\frac{c}{2}=5]].')} /> Using 5 for <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=5\\times12=60]]')} /> instead of 24. Fix: ask whether the circle sits inside the triangle or passes through its vertices.</p>

  <h3>2. Using the Full Perimeter Instead of the Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>The formula needs <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, half the perimeter. With <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> and full perimeter 24 instead of <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />, the result is <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times24=48]]')} />, twice the correct 24. Fix: halve the perimeter before multiplying.</p>

  <h3>3. Dividing the Final Answer by Two Again</h3>
  <p>The halving is already inside <span set:html={rich('[[s]].')} /> Dividing <span set:html={rich('[[2\\times12=24]]')} /> by two again gives 12, half the true area. Fix: calculate <span set:html={rich('[[rs]]')} /> and stop.</p>

  <h3>4. Confusing the Incenter With the Circumcenter</h3>
  <p>The incenter is where angle bisectors meet; the circumcenter is where perpendicular bisectors meet, and for a right triangle it sits at the hypotenuse midpoint. They are different points. Fix: trace the correct construction for the circle you need.</p>

  <h3>5. Treating the Inradius as a Triangle Side</h3>
  <p>The inradius is a distance inside the triangle, not one of its three sides. It never belongs in <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c]].')} /> Fix: keep <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> out of every side-length expression.</p>

  <h3>6. Using an Incorrect Right-Triangle Inradius Formula</h3>
  <p>The right-triangle shortcut is <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />, not a variant with the wrong sign or the wrong side halved. A common slip is using <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+c-b}{2}]]')} />, which gives a different number unless the triangle is isosceles. Fix: the subtracted side is always the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></p>

  <h3>7. Forgetting That c Is the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>In the shortcut, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> means the hypotenuse specifically. Fix: label the longest side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> before applying the formula.</p>

  <h3>8. Using the Wrong Side as the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Picking a leg for <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> corrupts both <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]].')} /> Fix: the hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, always the longest.</p>

  <h3>9. Mixing Different Measurement Units</h3>
  <p>An inradius of 20 cm with a semiperimeter of 1 m gives no meaningful number until conversion. Fix: convert to one unit first, then multiply.</p>

  <h3>10. Entering Zero or Negative Lengths</h3>
  <p>Zero and negative lengths describe no triangle. Fix: check that every length entered is positive.</p>

  <h3>11. Assuming Every Positive r and s Pair Represents a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>As shown earlier, <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[s=10]]')} /> yields the number 20 but no right triangle. Fix: run the <span set:html={rich('[[s/r\\geq3+2\\sqrt2]]')} /> check when the geometry matters.</p>

  <h3>12. Reporting Area in Linear Units</h3>
  <p>An area of 24 m² is not 24 m. Fix: write the squared unit with the number.</p>

  <h3>13. Rounding the Inradius Too Early</h3>
  <p>Rounding <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> before multiplying shifts the whole area. Fix: keep full precision through the intermediate steps and round only the final answer.</p>

  <h3>14. Confusing Semiperimeter With the Sum of Two Sides</h3>
  <p>The semiperimeter is half the sum of all three sides, not the sum of two. Fix: add all three sides, then halve.</p>

  <h3>15. Assuming the Formula Only Works for Right Triangles</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> holds for every non-degenerate triangle; only the shortcut <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> is right-triangle specific. Fix: apply the area formula freely, but use the general inradius derivation for non-right triangles.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Use a 6-8-10 triangle. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[r=2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times12=24]].')} /> Incorrectly using the full perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times24=48]]')} />, which doubles the area. Incorrectly dividing the correct answer by two again: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{24}{2}=12]]')} />, which halves it. Both errors come from mishandling the one-half that lives inside <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /></p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Right Triangle Area Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Several independent checks catch the common errors. The 3-4-5 triangle demonstrates most of them.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Inradius and Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Recompute <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> with the entered values. For <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6=6]].')} /> A second pass catches typos.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Two Perpendicular Legs</h3>
  <p>When the legs are known or recovered, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> must agree. Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} /> matches <span set:html={rich('[[1\\times6=6]]')} />: two methods, one triangle, one answer.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]].')} /> If the sides do not satisfy it, the triangle identification is wrong.</p>

  <h3>Method 4: Right Triangle Inradius</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3+4-5}{2}=1]].')} /> A mismatch means the entered inradius does not belong to this triangle.</p>

  <h3>Method 5: Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6]].')} /> This catches mix-ups between perimeter and semiperimeter.</p>

  <h3>Method 6: Heron's Formula</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6\\times3\\times2\\times1}=6]].')} /> Agreement between independent formulas is the strongest check available.</p>
  <p>Explain why agreement between independent formulas is a useful mathematical check: a mistake in one route rarely reproduces itself in another, so two matching answers from different formulas mean both the inputs and the arithmetic are sound.</p>

