<!-- Section 15: Measurement units and rounding -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Measurement Units in Inradius and Semiperimeter Calculations</h2>
  <p>Area is two-dimensional, so its unit is the square of the length unit. In symbols, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{cm}\\times\\text{cm}=\\text{cm}^2]].')} /> The same holds for metres and square metres, millimetres and square millimetres, feet and square feet, inches and square inches. The unit selectors exist so the result can be labelled correctly.</p>

  <h3>Why Both Inputs Must Use Compatible Units</h3>
  <p>The inradius and the semiperimeter must be expressed in the same unit before the formula is applied. If <span set:html={rich('[[r=20\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=1\\text{ m}]]')} />, convert first: <span set:html={rich('[[1\\text{ m}=100\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=20\\times100]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=2,000\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />, or equivalently <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=0.2\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. Multiplying measurements in different units produces a number with no clean unit, so convert first and calculate second.</p>

  <h3>Why You Should Avoid Early Rounding</h3>
  <p>Rounded values of <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> may slightly affect the final area, because the inradius is multiplied directly into it. Keep sufficient precision through the intermediate calculations and round only the final answer. This guide makes no claim about the calculator's internal precision beyond what its interface shows.</p>

  <h3>Exact Measurements Versus Practical Measurements</h3>
  <p>Exact mathematical dimensions, like the inradius 1 of a 3-4-5 triangle, behave differently from measurements taken with physical instruments. Instrument uncertainty flows into the area, so treat the last digit as only as reliable as the inputs. A tape-measured inradius of 2 cm might really be 1.9 or 2.1, and the area carries that uncertainty with it.</p>
</section>

